questions.questionHtml:
Let $$A = \left( {\matrix{
   1 &amp; 2  \cr 
   3 &amp; 4  \cr 

 } } \right)$$ and $$B = \left( {\matrix{
   a &amp; 0  \cr 
   0 &amp; b  \cr 

 } } \right),a,b \in N.$$ Then

questions.explanationText:
$$A = \left[ {\matrix{
1 & 2 \cr
3 & 4 \cr

} } \right]\,\,\,\,B = \left[ {\matrix{
a & 0 \cr
0 & b \cr

} } \right]$$

$$AB = \left[ {\matrix{
a & {2b} \cr
{3a} & {4b} \cr

} } \right]$$

$$BA = \left[ {\matrix{
a & 0 \cr
0 & b \cr

} } \right]\left[ {\matrix{
1 & 2 \cr
3 & 4 \cr

} } \right] = \left[ {\matrix{
a & {2a} \cr
{3b} & {4b} \cr

} } \right]$$

Hence, $$AB=BA$$ only when $$a=b$$

$$\therefore$$ There can be infinitely many $$B's$$

for which $$AB=BA$$

